 "@type": "Quiz",
 "name": "Labour Markets — Exam Questions & Model Answers",
 "about": {
  "@type": "Thing",
  "name": "Edexcel IAL Economics 3.3.4 Labour Markets"
 },
 "educationalLevel": "International A Level",
 "hasPart": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Explain (4 marks)",
   "text": "Explain what is meant by the derived demand for labour.",
   "answerCount": 1,
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Derived demand means that labour is not demanded for its own sake but because it is needed to produce goods and services that consumers want . K The demand for workers depends on the demand for the product they make — if product demand rises, firms need more workers to increase output. K For example, if demand for electric vehicles increases, car manufacturers like Tesla will hire more production workers, engineers, and battery technicians — the demand for these workers is derived from consumer demand for EVs. A Conversely, falling demand for print newspapers has reduced the derived demand for journalists and printers in that industry. A"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Examine (8 marks)",
   "text": "Examine how a monopsony employer might lead to lower wages and employment.",
   "answerCount": 1,
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "A monopsony is a labour market with a single or dominant employer . Unlike a competitive market where firms are wage-takers, a monopsonist has wage-setting power . K The NHS is often cited as a monopsony employer of nurses in the UK — in many regions, the NHS is the only realistic employer for qualified nursing staff. A The monopsonist faces an upward-sloping labour supply curve — to attract more workers, it must offer higher wages. An Crucially, when it raises the wage for the new worker, it must also raise wages for all existing workers . This means the marginal cost of labour (MCL) is higher than the wage — the cost of hiring one more worker includes both their wage and the wage increase for everyone already employed. An The profit-maximising monopsonist employs workers up to the point where MCL = MRP (marginal revenue product). An However, it then pays the wage on the supply curve — which is lower than MRP. D The result is that both the wage and employment level are lower than they would be in a competitive labour market, where the wage equals MRP. An Workers are paid less than the value of their contribution, and the monopsonist captures the difference as additional profit. An"
   }
  }
 ]
